WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.470
Welcome back to part two, your second
lesson of these seven lessons. If you

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.340
haven't yet subscribed to this channel,
Communication Coach, I encourage you to

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.120
do so at some point in the process.
Because, in addition to this free course

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.369
there's lots of other material on the channel and I keep adding more material every

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:21.337
week. So let's jump into lesson number
two.

00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:26.760
In our first lesson, we looked at

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.869
long-windedness. In this one we're
going to get after how to form a concise

00:00:30.869 --> 00:00:36.840
overall message. So, concise tip number
two is keep the overall message tight.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.430
Mark Twain once joked I didn't have time
to write a short letter so I wrote a

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.140
long one instead.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.649
And the point is making there in a
humorous way is that it takes time to

00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:51.539
whittle down your message to the essence. It's much easier to just be long-winded.

00:00:51.539 --> 00:00:56.280
But, everybody appreciates a concise
message, whether it's a presentation,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.879
meeting, a talking turning in a
conversation, or your elevator pitch,

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.650
people want you to finish on time and
they want you to wrap it up. I put

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.420
elevator pitch and quotations because,
first of all, I don't like that phrase but also

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.860
its signals that it's a message that you
have to finish in about the time you

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.549
have an elevator ride. So you have to
express what you do or what your

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:21.060
organization does and how they add value in a very short amount of time.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:26.850
Strategy number one is skip to the
heart of the message. That's the best way

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.470
to compress your overall message. Skip
the preamble, the qualifications, the

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:36.509
backstory. Don't think out loud, just
bottom-line it. This is a picture there of a

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:40.890
golden retriever. I've a little golden
retriever puppy right now. This reminded

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:41.759
me of her.

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.649
So, what's the heart of this message? So I
was driving around the other day doing,

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:50.880
you know, errands and what not. I saw a car go by they look just like yours, at least

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.930
it looked like your car to me. Do you drive a Toyota? Anyway, I saw this car

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:55.770
and it made me think of you.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:01.200
You know what? It may have been a Honda,
not a Toyota. SUVs looks so similar. Long

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.740
story short, it made me think of you and
that's why I called. It's been too long.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.530
We'd love to get together. We're
wondering if you'd like to bring your

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.060
family over next weekend.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.090
It might have been a Subaru.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:17.030
So, if you have to whittle this down to
the very core part of this, the part

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.930
that you absolutely want the other
person to hear out of this message,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.959
what would it be? If it were me, I would
say it's been too long.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:27.110
We'd love to get together. We're
wondering if you'd like to bring your

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:31.310
family over next weekend. If you got off
the phone and didn't say these three

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:35.480
sentences, I think that you probably
would have said I never really said what

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.260
I wanted to say. If you take away the
rest of it, you have the heart of the

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:43.430
message. The opposite is not true. If you
took away these three sentences and

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:47.269
you're just left with the rambling about
the car, the message would make very

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:51.530
little sense. And so you want to make
sure you focus on what the person needs

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.209
to know and we do that by using need-to-know versus nice-to-know thinking to get

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.530
to the heart of the message.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.790
What does the person absolutely need to
know and what's just nice to know, little

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.930
details that you could leave out and still
communicate your message effectively?

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.410
If the person is, let's say they're stressed
for time or your reception is not good

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:10.790
on the phone,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.340
what would you focus on? That's generally when you're going to get down to the

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.019
heart of the message. Strategy number two is to make your point and then zip it.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:21.200
So you state the heart of the message.
End you're talking turn by literally

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.350
closing your mouth. And give the other
person a chance to talk.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.700
Look for those head nods and other
nonverbal cues that shows that that

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:34.010
person is reading you and you stop
talking after just a few sentences. So

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:39.109
let's say you're having a Q&amp;A with
somebody and they ask you have you

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:43.880
contacted meeting planner yet? What would you cut out of the answer to get to the

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.560
heart of the message then zip it?

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.419
Then close your mouth? Here's the answer. No, not yet. I still have to figure out

00:03:50.419 --> 00:03:51.380
what we need from her.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.930
I've got a call scheduled with the
children ministry leader but I have to

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.769
see if that call is actually going to
happen. I'm also waiting to hear back

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:01.430
from the musicians. If our first
choice for music is available then that will

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.569
shape the rest of the program. There are
lots of balls in the air right now but

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:06.470
we'll get there.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.739
I just didn't anticipate so many loose
strings, you know, that would still be

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:13.760
loose at this point. So remember this is
a very direct question. Have you

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.919
contacted the meeting planner? This is a
yes or no. For me, I would cut almost all

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:22.670
this to make sure I express my main
point and then zip it.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:28.100
No, not yet. I still have to figure out
what we need from her. And this leaves

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:32.120
room for the conversation to continue. So, now it's their turn to talk. And

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.780
hopefully, you're noticing these
nonverbal cues that signal you to stop.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.400
A lot of times, people misread this. The
person says Okay, all right. They're nodding.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.720
And we might think, Oh they really like
what I'm saying. I'll keep talking, but it

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.060
means just the opposite. This is your
signal to stop. Strategy number three.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.910
Finish just under time. In many
situations like a meeting you'll be

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.530
given a certain amount of time for that
work. And you want to wrap it up under

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:04.130
the time limit. So, if you have an hour
wrap that meeting up at 55 minutes.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:08.360
Nobody's going to complain. You have a
30-minute presentation, finish in 28

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:14.030
minutes. If you ask somebody for five
minutes of the time only use four. Develop

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:19.640
a habit of getting under time instead of
going over time. And a lot of people go

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.570
over time, especially in meetings and
presentations and conversations like

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.350
this. In fact, you might take a great
speaker that you think is amazing and

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:32.180
she's just crushing it at the 25-minute
mark and she's supposed to finish at 30

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:38.601
minutes. And she doesn't. And now she's 35 or 40, 45. And now a speaker that was amazing

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:43.130
a few minutes ago is now 10 or 15 minutes overtime. And they're boring

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:44.630
everybody to death.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:48.080
You do not want to be that kind of
communicator. You want to finish just

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.400
under time. Respect people's time and
they will start to think of you as a

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.160
concise communicator. So keep your
overall messages tight. To do that you

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.300
want to skip to the heart of the message.
I think most conversational messages can

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.350
be delivered about two or three
sentences only. Once you make your point,

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:09.320
zip it and give the other person a
chance to talk. That will develop into a

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.670
conversation. And when you have some kind of time limit that you're shooting for,

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:19.730
finished just under time. Your first task
is to apply this in conversations,

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:24.805
stories, and any kinds of meetings that
you have coming up in the near future.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:28.940
Up next, we're going to work on concise
and clear sentences and I look forward

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.960
to seeing you in that lesson.

